{"id": 14, "ts": 1784268148.4108744, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 1, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I agree with Agent\u202f1 that Candidate\u202fC remains the strongest overall fit, despite the concerns raised about crew cooperation. The alternative candidates each have more critical safety\u2011relevant gaps: Candidate\u202fA, while able to make quick correct decisions and stay calm in a crisis, is described as egocentric, has difficulty communicating ideas, and is not very willing to pursue further education \u2013 all of which undermine reliable crew interaction and continuous professional development. Candidate\u202fB, although very conscientious, handles stress well, and assesses weather expertly, also makes nasty remarks about his colleagues, indicating a clear deficit in crew cooperation, and his weak memory for numbers could jeopardise attention to detail in flight\u2011deck calculations. Candidate\u202fD concentrates well and solves problems, but is perceived as a know\u2011it\u2011all and has a hot temper, traits that risk deteriorating cockpit harmony on long\u2011haul flights. In contrast, Candidate\u202fC\u2019s ability to anticipate dangerous situations, see complex connections, and exhibit strong leadership directly supports technical competence, decision quality, and operational safety. While the show\u2011off perception and limited openness to new ideas are concerns, they appear less safety\u2011critical than the interpersonal deficiencies of the other candidates. Therefore, I maintain my provisional recommendation for Candidate\u202fC, pending any further evidence that might mitigate the crew\u2011cooperation concerns."}
